.Dd November 22, 2026
.Dt NVRM-INFO 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvrm-info
.Nd describe the NVIDIA GPUs through the Resource Manager
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -json | Fl -jsonl | Fl -schema
.Op Fl -backend Ar name
.Nm
.Fl h | Fl -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility lists every NVIDIA GPU with the values
.Xr nvrm 3
reports.
After it has opened its descriptors it enters capability mode and queries the
GPUs again through them; if capability mode is not available it fails.
The text output lists every value of the library, as the JSON does, and prints
the running driver, RM API, built-for RM, library, library-symbol and schema
version strings, and each GPU's VBIOS and GSP firmware version strings.
It needs no Linux compatibility layer.
.Bl -tag -width ".Fl -backend Ar name"
.It Fl -json
Print one JSON document on one line, schema
.Li nvrm-info/1 .
Every version is an object containing its preserved string and, when available,
its integer parts.
.It Fl -jsonl
Print JSON Lines: a header record, one record per GPU, an end record.
Every version has the same string-plus-integer-parts object as in
.Fl -json .
Output is flushed per line; a closed pipe ends the program quietly.
.It Fl -schema
Print the JSON Schema that every JSON mode validates against.
.It Fl -backend Ar name
.Dq rm
or
.Dq fake ;
the default is
.Ev NVRM_BACKEND ,
else the kernel driver.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage.
.El
.Pp
In the JSON modes a failure prints one error record on standard output.
Values that cannot be read are
.Li "available": false
with a reason, never a made-up number.
.Ss Versions
Document and header records contain a
.Li versions
object with
.Li driver ,
.Li rm_api ,
.Li built_for ,
.Li library ,
.Li library_symbols
and
.Li schema
members.
An available version contains
.Li available ,
.Li string ,
.Li major ,
.Li minor ,
.Li patch ,
the complete
.Li parts
array and
.Li source .
A non-empty
.Li suffix
is also present.
An unavailable version instead contains
.Li available ,
.Li string ,
.Li reason_id ,
.Li reason
and
.Li source ,
with no part fields.
The legacy
.Li built_for_rm_version
and
.Li running_rm_version
members are unchanged.
.Ss GPU versions
Each device record has a
.Li versions
group containing
.Li vbios
and
.Li gsp_firmware
version objects, both with source
.Li rm .
The VBIOS object combines the RM revision and OEM revision and parses its five
hexadecimal fields in base 16.
The GSP firmware object is parsed in base 10 when the RM marks it valid.
A failed control gives reason
.Li rm_control_failed ;
When the RM does not run on the GPU System Processor, or does not mark its
firmware version valid, the reason is
.Li rm_not_supported .
While an RM client holds a GPU open, the OS layer exposes the same values as
the
.Va hw.nvidia.gpus.N.vbios_*
and
.Va hw.nvidia.gpus.N.firmware_*
sysctls.
With no client holding the GPU open, those sysctls read
.Li N/A
or
.Li ?? .
.Pp
.Li rm_sm_version
is the raw RM SM version code, and
.Li sm_version_major
and
.Li sm_version_minor
are the RM's own numbering from that code.
They are not necessarily CUDA numbers.
.Li compute_capability_major
and
.Li compute_capability_minor
are derived only from the architecture and implementation table.
With no table row they are unavailable with reason
.Li family_unknown ;
an alias or a raw RM code is never presented as a compute capability.
Thus a GB206 can report RM SM 10.4 and compute capability 12.0, while GB20B has
compute capability 12.1.
.Ss Operating-system values
The running driver version comes from these sysctls when the kernel module
provides the complete set:
.Bl -tag -width ".Va hw.nvidia.version_available" -compact
.It Va hw.nvidia.version_string
The preserved short version string.
.It Va hw.nvidia.version_major
The integer major part.
.It Va hw.nvidia.version_minor
The integer minor part.
.It Va hw.nvidia.version_patch
The integer patch part.
.It Va hw.nvidia.version_suffix
The suffix, including its leading delimiter, or an empty string.
.It Va hw.nvidia.version_available
1 when the version has a readable numeric scheme, otherwise 0.
.El
.Pp
The integer parts and suffix must agree with the parsed version string.
Any disagreement makes the driver version unavailable with reason
.Li version_inconsistent
and preserves the version string.
A zero
.Va hw.nvidia.version_available
also makes it unavailable.
.Pp
On an older module without that set,
.Nm
reads the banner from
.Va hw.nvidia.version
after probing its length, reads up to 64 KiB, and finds its first numeric
version token.
The legacy running-version text contains only the short version, never the
whole banner.
The RM API version is read separately with the RM version-query ioctl.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Ev NVRM_BACKEND ,
.Ev NVRM_FAKE_PROFILE
and
.Ev NVRM_FAKE_FAIL ;
see
.Xr nvrm 3 .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A machine with the driver loaded and no GPU is not a failure: the status is 0.
Text mode prints "no GPU is attached",
.Fl -json
prints an empty list of devices, and
.Fl -jsonl
prints the header and an end record with count 0.
No usable backend is a failure.
Status 2 means a usage error, including two output modes or
.Fl -schema
with
.Fl -backend .
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print the header record and the first GPU as JSON Lines:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nvrm-info --jsonl | head -2
.Ed
.Pp
Print the schema the JSON output validates against:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nvrm-info --schema
.Ed
.Pp
Run on a machine without a GPU, against synthetic data:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
NVRM_BACKEND=fake nvrm-info --json
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nvrm 3 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr nvrm 7
